1 NOTE Knut Knudson Mørkve, aged 26, his wife, Anna Larsdtr., aged 30, and their daughter Ingeborg, 1½, left Voss for the Parish of Hadsel in Nordland County (Norway), according to certificate from the Pastor of August 14, 1838. In 1870 
2 CONC they left for America with their children, excepting son Knute who had married Mathea Olsdtr. Ostvoldalen, daughter Ingeborg who had married Hans Andreas Olson Reine (Knute had gone in 1868), Olina, and Guri who was not well left 
2 CONC in an asylum. There is question as to whether or not there was a daughter, Olina. The Rene history of emigration from Voss lists a daughter, Olina, as does the emigration register for Hordaland (which includes Voss), although ther
2 CONC e are no birth or emigration dates given, as is the case with her other siblings who were born in North Norway.  If there really was an Olina, she would have been born in North Norway, and then may have married and emigrated to th
2 CONC e US with her new family.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The Minnesota state census of 1875 shows Knut and Anna living with son John in Bath township, Freeborn county, north of Albert Lea, MN. Listed are: John M. Knutson(25), wife Anna H.(21), daughter Clara T.(1), brother Olaus(28)
2 CONC , father Knut Knutson(65), mother Anna Knutson(69), and brother Casper(29). Knut and Anna bought 40 acres about 2 miles northwest of Fertile in Worth County, IA.  Their house was built of logs on the bottom story and cut lumber on
2 CONC  the top.  When K. O. Sheimo bought the property about 1900, the top was brought to the Sheimo property (1/2 mile west) and used for a granary. The foundation was built with laid-up lime rock, probably brought from near-by sources
2 CONC .  The land was good, except that it was on the edge of a peat bog and was sometimes wet.  Wheat was probably the primary crop taken to the nearby mill still standing in Fertile.  They also grew buckwheat for pancakes and a little
2 CONC  corn, hay, and oats.
2 CONT
2 CONT      When Knut died in 1894, un-married son Olaus farmed the property for about four years.  His brother, Casper, bought the northwest quarter of the same section in 1887, and after Olaus sold Knut's 40 in 1898 to K.O. Skjemo, Ola
2 CONC us and mother Anna lived with Casper for about 2 years. Casper sold half his land in 1898 and the rest in 1900.  The 1900 census shows Casper, Olaus and Anna living there before June, 1900. Sometime after that, Casper moved to Mon
2 CONC tana, and Olaus moved to North Dakota.  Olaus was living with his niece, Jenny (Reine) Nelson at Milaca, MN at the time of his death. It is not know when Anna died or where.  Knut's oldest son, Knute, had come to America before th
2 CONC e rest of his family, and he lived in the same area before he moved to North Dakota.  His wife had brothers in Worth county, IA (Peter and Andreas Oswald).
2 CONT
2 CONT      There is also much information known about Hauga Knut's siblings and ancestors in possession of Richard Flisrand, Austin, MN.
