2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [March2004transfer.ged]   EMIGRATED: Emigrated ABT 1873 from Norway   Per the 1880 Dakota Census: Highland township, Minnehaha county, Dakota: Peter Anderson, age 27, born Norway. Martha Anderson, age 34, born Norway. Anders Ander
2 CONC son, age 6, born Minnesota Ole Anderson, age 4, born Dakota Bertine (assume your Anne Bertine) Anderson, age 3, born Dakota Anton Anderson, age 1, born Dakota.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Highland Lutheran Church and Cemetery is located in the back corner of the Fjellanger farm. It was built after the Fjellanger family donated the land to the church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Norwegian Pioneers: History of Minnehaha County, South Dakota, 1866-1896": Peder Fjellanger was named as a trustee for the Landstads Congregation organized by a Paster S. J. Nummedahl in a conference on December 27, 1881. Be
2 CONC ndingsbo, Peder born 17th of October, 1845, Voss, Bergens diocese, Norway, Martha born Fjellanger, 31st of July, 1845, Voss, Bergens diocese, Norway, 9 children: Andrew, Ole, Bertha, Anton, Dinah, Martha, Martha (#2), Simon and Ja
2 CONC kob. They emigrated to America in 1873 and came to Fillmore County, Minnesota, where they resided for 2 years. Peder was out here to Minnehaha County and selected a Homestead in 1874, but moved on it the first of next Spring. They
2 CONC  were not rich when they started to make preparations for their home, as there was to be found only $14.00 in the money bag, but with diligence and hard work it went steadily forward, so that when Mr. Fjellanger died in 1920, they
2 CONC  were owners of 320 acres in section 17, in Highland Township. The first year they were in their home they had only two sacks of ground corn to live on from the 1st of March and to the end of July. When the severe snowstorm of Jan
2 CONC uary 12, 1888 came, Mr. Fjellanger lay ill of rheumatic fever, 2 of his sons Andrew and Anton were out watering the cattle a good way from home. They had to leave the cattle a good way from home. They had to leave the cattle and c
2 CONC ome back to the house and only one calf was saved out of the 17 livestock. The others were found frozen to death the next day out on the range, a good mile from the home. The future didn't look too good now that the father and one
2 CONC  son lay ill, together with the lost livestock, but with good neighbors help and good will, they soon began again; some gave a cow, others a calf or a heifer and so they started anew. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
