1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DNA marker R-L238
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars Johnsen Bjørke (b. 1766), known as “Store Bjørke” (Large/Great Bjørke), was a prominent and prosperous farmer on Voss in western Norway. ￼
2 CONT He married Ingeborg Nilsdatter Jørdre (b. 1772). Together they built a life of notable wealth and enterprise in an era when most Norwegian farmers lived modestly. ￼
2 CONT Acquiring and Expanding the Farm
2 CONT In 1788, at around age 22, Lars received a deed from his father, John Larsen, for a part of the Bjørke farm, along with a one-third share in a sawmill and a stampe (a water-powered facility for processing leather, homespun cloth, 
2 CONC and similar materials). The price was 600 Daler. He later inherited the rest of his father’s farm. ￼
2 CONT Bjørke (meaning something like “birch” farm, from bjørk) was already an established holding. Lars’s additional properties included:
2 CONT •  Three parts of land by the shores (likely along Lake Vossavatnet/Vangsvatnet).
2 CONT •  The farm called Olavsholmen.
2 CONT This made him one of the largest landowners in the area. ￼
2 CONT Wealth and Enterprises
2 CONT “Store Bjørke” earned his nickname as one of the richest farmers on Voss. His operations went well beyond basic subsistence farming:
2 CONT •  Livestock trading: He owned multiple horses and cattle. He sold them in the bustling market of Bergen (the major coastal city) or lent them to other local farmers on Voss, generating income and building social/economic ties.
2 CONT •  Distilling: He produced spirits (likely aquavit or similar) supplied to the guest house at Kongstun for 12 shillings per bottle — a steady commercial venture.
2 CONT •  Fishing and aquaculture: He ran fishing operations and cultivated fish (trout and probably Arctic char) in local lakes, adding another revenue stream from the region’s abundant waters. ￼
2 CONT These diversified activities — agriculture, milling/processing, animal husbandry and trade, distilling, and fishing — reflect a shrewd, entrepreneurial spirit typical of successful storbønder (large farmers) in 18th- and early 19t
2 CONC h-century Norway.
2 CONT Historical Context
2 CONT Lars lived during a transitional period in Norwegian history. Born under the Denmark-Norway union, he saw the Napoleonic Wars’ aftermath, including the 1814 shift to a union with Sweden. Population growth (aided by smallpox vaccin
2 CONC ation) increased pressure on land, but enterprising farmers like him could thrive through trade and multiple income sources. Voss, with its strategic location and fertile valleys, supported such success. ￼
2 CONT His son, Lars Larsen Bjørke (b. ~1815/1816), continued the family legacy on related farms (including connections to Graue/Grauo). Many of Lars Johnsen’s grandchildren later emigrated to North America amid broader 19th-century emig
2 CONC ration waves from Voss driven by land scarcity and economic opportunity. ￼
2 CONT Lars Johnsen Bjørke exemplified the peak of traditional Norwegian rural prosperity: hardworking, innovative within the constraints of the time, and deeply rooted in the land and waters of Voss. His story, preserved in local geneal
2 CONC ogies and forums like Norway Heritage, highlights how a capable farmer could rise to prominence through smart inheritance, diversification, and trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lars Johnsen Bjørke b. 1766 married Ingeborg Nilsdatter Jørdre b. 1772, recievd deed on a part in Bjørke, 1/3 in a saw mill and a "Stampe" Stampe was a facility driven by water for processing leather, homespun etc. for 600 Daler f
2 CONC rom his father John Larsen in 1788, but recieved the rest of his fathers farm later. Lars Johnsen also owned three parts of land by the shores (at lake Vossavatnet?) and a farm called Olavsholmen. Lars Johnsen was called "Store Bj
2 CONC ørke" Large Bjørke and was one of the richest farmers on Voss.  He owned several horses and cattle he sold in Bergen or lent to other farmers on Voss. He also produced spirit to the guest house Kongstun for 12 Shilling a bottle an
2 CONC d ran fishing and fish cultivation in some lakes of trout and probably char.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Akre of the forestry service has written in a report that in Bjørkemdn (the Bjørku forest) are the largest and finest pine trees to be found on the West Coast. The pines have large top measurements over great lengths and much
2 CONC  has been sold as mast timber in the age of sailing ships and for boat building. The largest pine in Bjørkemdn was protected in 1935. It is on holding 3, 2.40 meters in circumference at chest height, approximately 30 meters tall, 
2 CONC and is said to be the finest pine tree on Voss, says a forester well acquainted with the forests. Nils Larsson gave the pine to the grandchildren named Nils. Of these Lars Nilsson b. 1847 bought the pine for 200 kronor.
2 CONT Store-Bjørken (Lars Jonson), when he had guests and they had come into the sitting room, had the habit of accompanying them out into the farmyard, pointing down toward Bjørkemdn and saying: 'There you see my shop.'
2 CONT Many of the Bjørku men have been particularly protective of their forest. It is told of one that when he came in the morning with his workers to the forest, he would walk around tree after tree and finally come to one they were ab
2 CONC out to fell. But when he then looked up at the tree, he found it too good. In this way he sometimes went until midday without finding anything that was prefered. 
2 CONT
