1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/37144122/orvin-herbert-ramlo
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Iowa the son of Clarence Ramlo 1890-1940 and Olena Bakken 1889-1958. Orvin married Setma Ida Kuhnert 1915-2000 and they had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT A WWII Ace credited with shooting down Two "Betty" bombers, three "Zero" fighters, plus two other aircraft.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flying a Grumman F4F Wildcat he was wounded in the battle of Guadalcanal in September of 1942.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second Lieutenant Orvin Herbert Ramlo (MCSN: 0-9383), United States Marine Corps, was awarded the Distinguished Flying Cross for extraordinary achievement while participating in aerial flight as Pilot of a Fighter Airplane in Mari
2 CONC ne Fighting Squadron TWO HUNDRED TWENTY-THREE (VMF-223), in action against enemy Japanese forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flying a Chance-Vought SB2U Vindicator he further distinguished himself in the battle of Midway in June of 1942. He was flying one of eleven aircraft under the command of Major Benjamin W Norris, Major Norris did not survive. In a
2 CONC  bomb run on the Battleship Haruna Orvin made a direct hit causing extensive damage. In return he had a disturbing number of holes punched in his aircraft; his gunner, Private Teman Wilhite, was wounded in four places and struggle
2 CONC d to stay conscious. On his return to Midway his aircraft was deemed unflyable. His was the only U.S. squadron to see combat with the SB2U.
2 CONT
2 CONT RAMLO, ORVIN HERBERT
2 CONT Citation:
2 CONT The President of the United States takes pleasure in presenting the Navy Cross to Orvin Herbert Ramlo (0-9383), Second Lieutenant, U.S. Marine Corps (Reserve), for extraordinary heroism and distinguished service in the line of his
2 CONC  profession while serving as a Pilot in Marine Scout-Bombing Squadron TWO HUNDRED FORTY-ONE (VMSB-241), Marine Air Group TWENTY-TWO (MAG-22), Naval Air Station, Midway, during operations of the U.S. Naval and Marine Forces against
2 CONC  the invading Japanese Fleet during the Battle of Midway on 4 and 5 June 1942. During the initial attack upon an enemy aircraft carrier, Second Lieutenant Ramlo, in a hail of blasting fire from Japanese fighter guns and anti-aircr
2 CONC aft batteries, dived his plane to the perilously low altitude of four hundred feet before releasing his bomb. His courageous determination and extreme disregard of personal safety were in keeping with the highest traditions of the
2 CONC  United States Naval Service.
2 CONT Approved by the Secretary of the Navy on November 10, 1942
2 CONT
2 CONT In civilian life he was a supervisor with North American Aviation and was also a very successful real-estate investor.
