1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/127317867/ida_christine-highland
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Grandma Highland's Hotel by Bob Edison:  The Highland Hotel in Mission Hill was very unique in that it was the only one in town.  It's a proprietor Ida Highland was a robust, heavy set Norwegian lady and the mother of fifteen chil
2 CONC dren, nine girls and six boys.  Her cooking skills were legendary and her home made bread and doughnuts attracted boarders from a wide area.
2 CONT A special admirer of her cooking was the sheriff of Yankton County, Bill Limpo, a throwback of pioneer days, who wore knee length boots with tassles, a vest adorned with a huge silver star, and a large black stetson hat.  Very imp
2 CONC osing!  He was a steady customer who regularly left grandma's hotel with two loaves of bread tucked under each arm!
2 CONT Grandma's customers were a motley lot, some of whom roomed ant boarded there and others, mostly bachelors, ate their meals at the hotel.  And some were characters!
2 CONT For instance, there was "crazy August", a German immigrant who spoke no English and who talked to himself a lot and who had a very bad temper.  Grandma could communicate with him somehow, although no one else could.  As kids we te
2 CONC ased him mercilessly which provoked him into a rage, which was, of course, our intent!  We assumed that he cussed us in German, but we weren't sure.  Years later, as a staff member of the Yankton State Hospital, I was to see Augus
2 CONC t there.
2 CONT And there was Pat Dineen, an irascible Irishman, who walked with a pronounced limb and used a cane.  He was perpetually short of breath because of his excess weight and he panted a lot.  He too had a short fuse and we kids went ou
2 CONC t of our way to irritate him!  He was our resident barber who used squeeze-type shears that pulled out our hair more often than cutting it.  He also placed his heavy hand upon our heads so that at the end of the shearing we had a 
2 CONC headache.  Bowl hair cuts were his speciality.
2 CONT A frequent customer of Grandma's was a Watkins Product salesman who was of Irish descent and always nattily attired and he had gold plated dentures-uppers and lowers.  What a smile!  He regularly buttonholed Grandma to sell his wa
2 CONC res out of his suitcase.  His favorite expression was:  "Can I put you down for a bottle of this?"  Grandma couldn't resist!
2 CONT Meals at Grandma's hotel were served on long tables in the dining room which was separated by a swinging door which caused some confusion in the coming and going.  Kids were not allowed there while the "men" were being served!  An
2 CONC d in the kitchen was a chest high table where meals were prepared and where the kids sat on stools to eat.  Grandma's meals certainly were not elaborate, but they were wholesome.  They were usually topped off with pie for dessert.
2 CONT Cooking was done on a wood fired range which, of course, had no thermosat or device to regulate the heat.  Recipes were unheard of and was usually done on a "pinch of this or a pinch of that" basis.  No attention was given to chol
2 CONC esterol or saturated fats!  Eat at your own risk!  Grandma always had a large pan of doughnuts in the pantry.
2 CONT Being too busy in the kitchen Grandma would send us to the store with a list of needed groceries.  Having had a minimum education she tended to spell words phonetically.  Thus on the list there was "bakon" and "bred", etc.  But we
2 CONC  knew what she meant and, when we came home with the groceries, we were the recipients of a bright, shiny nickel.  Our weekly allowance!
2 CONT Our family moved into Grandma's hotel and our mother helped with the daily chores there.  It was a building with high ceilings and it was poorly heated.  At bedtime we had heated irons placed in a sock and stuck in the foot of the
2 CONC  bed.  Sometimes late at night they would fall out making a loud "clunk" which awoke us.  On wash days which was done in the kitchen in the winter time we'd notice icicles up in the corners of the ceiling.  We would try to throw s
2 CONC omething at them to bring them down!
2 CONT An elongated three-in-one building stood behind the hotel which was the repository of cobs, wood, and coal while the middle building was the chicken house which gave refuge to Grandma's assortment of chickens.  So we always had eg
2 CONC gs and an occasional chicken dinner.  It was my job to behead the chickens which I did on the chopping block.  I'd fold their wings back and hold their legs on stretch out their necks.  My sisters, noting this and knowing what was
2 CONC  to follow would run back in the house.  One had to be a man about these undertakings!
2 CONT When circuses came to nearby Yankton, that building was plastered with advertising, which depicted clowns and elephants.  Grandma was then given free tickets for displays and, of course, there was competition for them amongst the 
2 CONC Edisons.  The posters remained on the building for years on end.
2 CONT So, Grandma Highland's hotel served as a mecca for wayward strangers, transients, and the regulars who were in need of substance and refugee.  It served a useful purpose but alas it gave way to changing times.  In its final gasp i
2 CONC t provided housing for a couple (Tom and Minnie Inch) but it was finally torn down leaving only an empty lot and, for those of us it sheltered, fond memories of those less complicated and innocent days.
