2 FORM png
2 TITL  Knud Gullakson Fadnas (1780-1857) - Find A Grave Memorial
1 NOTE Knut Gulleikson Fadness was born on May 4, 1780 on the Fadness farm near Evanger, Voss, Norway. He was the son of Gulleik Andersson Fadness and Torbjorg Knutsdatter Elgje. Knut married Ingeborg Olesdatter Rongen., in 1812,. She wa
2 CONC s born in 1791, the only surviving child of Ola Endreson Rongen and Anna Nilsdatter Botnen from Kvam. Knut and Ingeborg had at least eight children: Gulleik, Olav, Anna, Andreas, Nils, Endre, Haldor and Torbjorg. Ingeborg died in 
2 CONC 1847. She and her son Haldor, 1832-1832, are buried in Norway.<br>After a crop failure in 1847, Knut and three of his sons decided to emigrate to America. Knut`s second son, Olav, and his family together with Knut`s youngest broth
2 CONC er, Gulleik Gulleikson Fadness Vassend(en) (Gilbertson), and his family had already settled in 1845 on the Spring Prairie in what was to become Leeds Township, Columbia County, in the Territory of Wisconsin.<br>Shortly before leav
2 CONC ing Norway in 1848, Knut, age 68, married his first cousin-once removed, Inger Brynjulvsdatter Skjerveggen. Knut, Inger and Knut`s children: Andreas, Nils, Endre, Torbjorg and Anna with her husband Odd Brunborg, were joined by Knu
2 CONC t`s widowed sister, Mari Gulleiksdatter Fadness (Mrs Gulleik Andersson Skjerveggen {Gulleikson}) and her four children. They sailed on the ship Augusta from Bergen and arrived in New York on June 29, 1848. From here they traveled 
2 CONC up the Hudson River to the Erie Canal which took them over to the Great Lakes. At Milwaukee, these two families, along with others who came on the same ship from Norway, purchased oxen, wagons and supplies (depending on how much m
2 CONC oney they had left) and then walked about 100 miles to Spring Prairie.<br>On July 31, 1848, Knut bought a farm two miles north of his brother and son`s farms. This farm is located in Sections 13 and 24 in the Town of Leeds, Columb
2 CONC ia County, Wisconsin. Knut`s great-grandson, John Fadness and his wife, Karen, now own part of this farm. John and Karen received a Sesquicentennial Certificate this summer (1998) from the state of Wisconsin. The certificate says 
2 CONC that the same family has owned this farm since 1848 when Wisconsin became a state.<br>Knut purchased this farm from Sjur Reque, who moved to Koshkonong (Deerfield) in order to be near a church with a resident pastor; he had a daug
2 CONC hter old enough to take confirmation instruction. Two major community events had already occured on this farm. On March 7, 1847, the first Norwegian Lutheran church service led by a pastor in this community was held on this farm. 
2 CONC Three weeks later, March 28, 1847, Pastor J W C Dietrichson from Koshkonong returned to organize the Spring Prairie Norwegian Lutheran Congregation consisting of the Spring Prairie, Bonnet Prairie, and Norway Grove communities. Th
2 CONC is congregationwas part of the Koshkonong Parish. A cross was carved in a bur oak tree located in front of the log cabin and a table with a clean white tablecloth was placed under it to serve as the altar.<br>On October 15, 1849, 
2 CONC the Spring Prairie Congregation split from the Koshkonong Parish to become the Spring Prairie Parish consisting of three congretations,
