1 NOTE Knut O. Markve, born in Vossestrand, Norway, was the oldest son of Ole K. Mørkve.  (It is said that Ole was a fine silversmith.) Being the oldest son in the family meant that Knut inherited his father's farm, including a flour mil
2 CONC l on the falls.  He worked the farm for a time and then because he and Anna (Raustad), his wife, decided to go to America, he sold the farm to his brother Lars, who was called "Store Lars".  "Store" in Norwegian means big.  A youn
2 CONC ger brother was called "Vetla Lars" and "vetla" means small.  The same thing is true in Anna's family.  She was "Store Anna" and her sister was "Vetla Anna".  So Knuts moved to a different place for the time being, as Lars didn't 
2 CONC have money to pay them for the farm.  They waited 6 years for their money.  By this time they had 5 children: Ole, Andrew, Martha, Brita (died as an infant) and Bertha.
2 CONT      In 1883 they left Norway on the steamship "North Star" of the White Star Line, arriving in New York in June.  It was said they encountered a very bad storm on the trip across the ocean, so bad that they feared for their lives
2 CONC . Mother Anna had held all the children tight in her arms and, being a devout Christian, I am sure that there were many prayers prayed.
2 CONT      From New York they went by train to Arlington, SD, to the home of their brother and sister, Knut and Brita (Markve) Raustad, who lived on the south shore of Lake Albert in Badger Township, Kingsbury County.  A "40" west of Kn
2 CONC ut Raustad's had not been claimed, so July 10, 1883, when Knut made out his papers for filing for citizenship, he likely filed this homestead claim, because he paid for it one year later.
2 CONT      Knut Raustad was building a new house when they arrived so Markve's lived in that the first winter.  The unfinished house was very cold and Clara remembers her mother telling that it was so cold they nearly froze to death. Af
2 CONC ter the winter they moved to the "40" which contained a large slough.  They dug two shelters in the hillside of this slough, one for the barn and the other for a house.  Here Arthur was born in 1884.  Martha remembered running acr
2 CONC oss the field to get Mrs. Ola Stordahl at that time.  One spring, while living here, there were many fish to be caught in Lake Albert.  Knut filled his wagon-box and went to town to sell fish but others had been catching all the f
2 CONC ish they wanted too, so he didn't have a very profitable trip.  I wonder if they were brought home again and perhaps used for fertilizer.
2 CONT      Sometime in these years, Ingeri Markve, sister to Knut Markve and Brita (Markve) Raustad, came from Norway and stayed with Raustad's. Those in Norway that knew her remember her as being very timid and marveled that she could 
2 CONC make the trip alone.  They remembered her sister Olena in Norway, as being much more capable.
2 CONT      Knut had 2 cousins here, Lars and Ola Nesheim, who had come earlier from Norway and were living by Lake Preston, SD.  Lars was married in Norway to Ingebjorg Olsdtr. and they had 4 sons;  Knut, Oscar, Martin and John. Ole mar
2 CONC ried Ida Stordahl.  Their family was Melvin, Otis, Julia (Boe), Clara (Moen), Inman, Alfred, Arthur, Ansel, Clarence and Ernest.
2 CONT      There were still homesteads to be had in Faulk County, so Knut, Lars and Ola Nesheim decided to move there.  Before they moved, Knut sold the "40" to Knut Raustad.  Julia Boe told that these three walked to Faulkton, Faulk Co
2 CONC unty, and filed homestead claims north of Seneca. They later returned for their families.  Knut, Lars (Lewis on some official papers), Ola and Ingeri Markve, who also went along, all homesteaded within a mile of each other. Accord
2 CONC ing to the Faulk County record, they paid $1.25 an acre for these homesteads on April 2, 1886 (LeRoy Township).  Here in Faulk County, Otis and Julia Nesheim Boe were born in 1888 and 1890, and Clara Markve Thorsgaard was born in 
2 CONC 1888.
2 CONT      The six years spent in Faulk County were years of hardship.  In addition to establishing homes and farming, there were problems with draught and Indians. Bertha had said it was harder to wash dishes than to herd cows.  Every 
2 CONC other day they herded their cows seven miles to water them.  Martha told how a large pit was dug in the summer and partly filled with water to freeze in the winter.  They covered this so it wouldn't thaw in the summer and then the
2 CONC y kept their butter and milk here (a home-made refrigerator).
2 CONT      One day a messenger came telling them to go to town (Faulkton) as the Indians, led by Sitting Bull, were on the warpath.  The did leave. As it turned out, the Indians didn't get across the river but when Markve's returned hom
2 CONC e one of their two pigs had drowned in their barrel of precious rain water.  In Norway, the longest day of the year in June is called "St. Hans' Day" or "Midsummers Day".  Ingeri wasn't happy here and wanted to go back to Norway. 
2 CONC  On St. Hans' Day, she went to a fortune teller and asked if she should return to Norway or stay here and the fortune teller "read" the answer, "yes!".  She then sold the quarter section she homesteaded to her brother Knut, and we
2 CONC nt back to Norway.  From the records, we found that Knut sold it in 1888, so it must have been about that time that she went back. Ingeri spent her last years with Knut and Marta (Nesheim) Vethe (in Norway). Marta tells us that In
2 CONC geri was buried in Vinje cemetery and she doesn't think that her grave is marked.  The three remaining homesteaders and their families returned to eastern SD in 1891.  The Nesheims moved to the Lake Preston area (no Badger until 1
2 CONC 907) and Knut Markve's to the farm in Laketon township, Brookings County (SW 1/4, Section 18).  No record can be found in Brookings that they bought this Laketon farm. Being there such a short time, they likely rented.  Records sh
2 CONC ow that in 1885, it was recorded in Victor and Anna Erickson's name but was lost by mortgage in 1889.  In 1899 a Louis Larson deeded it to Maria Knutson.  She and her husband, Peter, were the parents of Mrs. Albert Raustad.  There
2 CONC  is a picture of the Knutson family on their silver anniversary and Carl Knutson's wedding day, 1904. Knutsons added the addition (to the right on the picture) after they bought it. Lilly Knutson-Peterson says that a "Flisrand" wa
2 CONC s along and helped build it (Andrew?).
2 CONT      In 1893, the Markves moved again, this time to Day County.  They bought a farm on the west side of the reservation line in Egeland township, which Ole bought from his father in about 1908.  At that time Knuts built a little h
2 CONC ome on the southwest side of Long Lake across the road from Ole's. While in Day County they still had their little problems with the Indians.  One time they had their wheat ready to thrash and after thrashing the first small load,
2 CONC  a storm and hard rain came and washed all the wheat down into the river.  Then the next day, the Indians came and they divided the little wheat they had thrashed with them.
2 CONT      During the summer of 1894, Clara, Martha, and their mother drove with a mule, named "Johnny" and a buggy to visit Knut Raustads and Nesheims in Kingsbury County.  This was a trip of about seventy miles one way. Darkness overt
2 CONC ook them when they were some distance south of Watertown and, coming to an empty house, they stopped there.  There was no other choice that night but to sleep on the floor.  "Johnny" was very tricky and could untie ropes.  So Mart
2 CONC ha tied his rope to her wrist as they slept, so that she would be awakened in case he tried to get away.
2 CONT      Knut and Anna lived in Day County the rest of Knut's life.  He passed away at the age of 92 on January 31, 1927.  Anna stayed with Oles until spring and at that time Clara and Arne Thorsgaard came and brought her to their hom
2 CONC e by Northwood, ND, which was her home the rest of her life. On the second day of Christmas before her ninetieth birthday, she broke the large bone in her upper leg and from that time on she was bedridden.  She was 96 1/2 years ol
2 CONC d when she died in 1944. Knut and Anna are buried in Helgen Cemetery, Codington County.  (by Alma Aaker Flisrand, 1983)
1 NOTE Knut O. Markve, born in Vossestrand, Norway, was the oldest son of Ole K. Mørkve.  (It is said that Ole was a fine silversmith.) Being the oldest son in the family meant that Knut inherited his father's farm, including a flour mil
2 CONC l on the falls.  He worked the farm for a time and then because he and Anna (Raustad), his wife, decided to go to America, he sold the farm to his brother Lars, who was called "Store Lars".  "Store" in Norwegian means big.  A youn
2 CONC ger brother was called "Vetla Lars" and "vetla" means small.  The same thing is true in Anna's family.  She was "Store Anna" and her sister was "Vetla Anna".  So Knuts moved to a different place for the time being, as Lars didn't 
2 CONC have money to pay them for the farm.  They waited 6 years for their money.  By this time they had 5 children: Ole, Andrew, Martha, Brita (died as an infant) and Bertha.
2 CONT      In 1883 they left Norway on the steamship "North Star" of the White Star Line, arriving in New York in June.  It was said they encountered a very bad storm on the trip across the ocean, so bad that they feared for their lives
2 CONC . Mother Anna had held all the children tight in her arms and, being a devout Christian, I am sure that there were many prayers prayed.
2 CONT      From New York they went by train to Arlington, SD, to the home of their brother and sister, Knut and Brita (Markve) Raustad, who lived on the south shore of Lake Albert in Badger Township, Kingsbury County.  A "40" west of Kn
2 CONC ut Raustad's had not been claimed, so July 10, 1883, when Knut made out his papers for filing for citizenship, he likely filed this homestead claim, because he paid for it one year later.
2 CONT      Knut Raustad was building a new house when they arrived so Markve's lived in that the first winter.  The unfinished house was very cold and Clara remembers her mother telling that it was so cold they nearly froze to death. Af
2 CONC ter the winter they moved to the "40" which contained a large slough.  They dug two shelters in the hillside of this slough, one for the barn and the other for a house.  Here Arthur was born in 1884.  Martha remembered running acr
2 CONC oss the field to get Mrs. Ola Stordahl at that time.  One spring, while living here, there were many fish to be caught in Lake Albert.  Knut filled his wagon-box and went to town to sell fish but others had been catching all the f
2 CONC ish they wanted too, so he didn't have a very profitable trip.  I wonder if they were brought home again and perhaps used for fertilizer.
2 CONT      Sometime in these years, Ingeri Markve, sister to Knut Markve and Brita (Markve) Raustad, came from Norway and stayed with Raustad's. Those in Norway that knew her remember her as being very timid and marveled that she could 
2 CONC make the trip alone.  They remembered her sister Olena in Norway, as being much more capable.
2 CONT      Knut had 2 cousins here, Lars and Ola Nesheim, who had come earlier from Norway and were living by Lake Preston, SD.  Lars was married in Norway to Ingebjorg Olsdtr. and they had 4 sons;  Knut, Oscar, Martin and John. Ole mar
2 CONC ried Ida Stordahl.  Their family was Melvin, Otis, Julia (Boe), Clara (Moen), Inman, Alfred, Arthur, Ansel, Clarence and Ernest.
2 CONT      There were still homesteads to be had in Faulk County, so Knut, Lars and Ola Nesheim decided to move there.  Before they moved, Knut sold the "40" to Knut Raustad.  Julia Boe told that these three walked to Faulkton, Faulk Co
2 CONC unty, and filed homestead claims north of Seneca. They later returned for their families.  Knut, Lars (Lewis on some official papers), Ola and Ingeri Markve, who also went along, all homesteaded within a mile of each other. Accord
2 CONC ing to the Faulk County record, they paid $1.25 an acre for these homesteads on April 2, 1886 (LeRoy Township).  Here in Faulk County, Otis and Julia Nesheim Boe were born in 1888 and 1890, and Clara Markve Thorsgaard was born in 
2 CONC 1888.
2 CONT      The six years spent in Faulk County were years of hardship.  In addition to establishing homes and farming, there were problems with draught and Indians. Bertha had said it was harder to wash dishes than to herd cows.  Every 
2 CONC other day they herded their cows seven miles to water them.  Martha told how a large pit was dug in the summer and partly filled with water to freeze in the winter.  They covered this so it wouldn't thaw in the summer and then the
2 CONC y kept their butter and milk here (a home-made refrigerator).
2 CONT      One day a messenger came telling them to go to town (Faulkton) as the Indians, led by Sitting Bull, were on the warpath.  The did leave. As it turned out, the Indians didn't get across the river but when Markve's returned hom
2 CONC e one of their two pigs had drowned in their barrel of precious rain water.  In Norway, the longest day of the year in June is called "St. Hans' Day" or "Midsummers Day".  Ingeri wasn't happy here and wanted to go back to Norway. 
2 CONC  On St. Hans' Day, she went to a fortune teller and asked if she should return to Norway or stay here and the fortune teller "read" the answer, "yes!".  She then sold the quarter section she homesteaded to her brother Knut, and we
2 CONC nt back to Norway.  From the records, we found that Knut sold it in 1888, so it must have been about that time that she went back. Ingeri spent her last years with Knut and Marta (Nesheim) Vethe (in Norway). Marta tells us that In
2 CONC geri was buried in Vinje cemetery and she doesn't think that her grave is marked.  The three remaining homesteaders and their families returned to eastern SD in 1891.  The Nesheims moved to the Lake Preston area (no Badger until 1
2 CONC 907) and Knut Markve's to the farm in Laketon township, Brookings County (SW 1/4, Section 18).  No record can be found in Brookings that they bought this Laketon farm. Being there such a short time, they likely rented.  Records sh
2 CONC ow that in 1885, it was recorded in Victor and Anna Erickson's name but was lost by mortgage in 1889.  In 1899 a Louis Larson deeded it to Maria Knutson.  She and her husband, Peter, were the parents of Mrs. Albert Raustad.  There
2 CONC  is a picture of the Knutson family on their silver anniversary and Carl Knutson's wedding day, 1904. Knutsons added the addition (to the right on the picture) after they bought it. Lilly Knutson-Peterson says that a "Flisrand" wa
2 CONC s along and helped build it (Andrew?).
2 CONT      In 1893, the Markves moved again, this time to Day County.  They bought a farm on the west side of the reservation line in Egeland township, which Ole bought from his father in about 1908.  At that time Knuts built a little h
2 CONC ome on the southwest side of Long Lake across the road from Ole's. While in Day County they still had their little problems with the Indians.  One time they had their wheat ready to thrash and after thrashing the first small load,
2 CONC  a storm and hard rain came and washed all the wheat down into the river.  Then the next day, the Indians came and they divided the little wheat they had thrashed with them.
2 CONT      During the summer of 1894, Clara, Martha, and their mother drove with a mule, named "Johnny" and a buggy to visit Knut Raustads and Nesheims in Kingsbury County.  This was a trip of about seventy miles one way. Darkness overt
2 CONC ook them when they were some distance south of Watertown and, coming to an empty house, they stopped there.  There was no other choice that night but to sleep on the floor.  "Johnny" was very tricky and could untie ropes.  So Mart
2 CONC ha tied his rope to her wrist as they slept, so that she would be awakened in case he tried to get away.
2 CONT      Knut and Anna lived in Day County the rest of Knut's life.  He passed away at the age of 92 on January 31, 1927.  Anna stayed with Oles until spring and at that time Clara and Arne Thorsgaard came and brought her to their hom
2 CONC e by Northwood, ND, which was her home the rest of her life. On the second day of Christmas before her ninetieth birthday, she broke the large bone in her upper leg and from that time on she was bedridden.  She was 96 1/2 years ol
2 CONC d when she died in 1944. Knut and Anna are buried in Helgen Cemetery, Codington County.  (by Alma Aaker Flisrand, 1983)
